WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.570 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  it's a summers day in 2012 and I'm  working on a community volunteering  project with my team from work I'm  painting a room in a small rundown house  and my boss Chris walks in and picks up  a brush the two of us chat back and  forth for a while before the  conversation takes a turn in a more  personal direction hey what's your  girlfriend's name  he asks Alana and and what does she do  and then how long have you guys been  together his questions keep coming and I  do my best to respond to them but my  heart is beginning to be faster and  faster why well because my partner's  name is not Alana its Alan and in  responding to Chris's questions I'm  growing this complex web of lies  to disguise Alan and my sexual  orientation from the view of my  colleagues and it's tough to keep up  have I answered that question before and  am I saying something now which  contradicts the things that I've said in  the past it's honestly exhausting but  this is what would run through my mind  every time anyone would ask me a  personal question at work even something  as innocuous as hey how's your weekend  would send a shiver up my spine well it  doesn't take much to understand that  this left me feeling like an outsider at  work in addition to that it caused my  productivity to plummet after all I was  spending so much time and energy trying  to keep the secret that there was little  energy left for me to deliver at work  furthermore I was throwing up these  barriers between my colleagues and I and  I avoided building any personal  relationships with them at all this is  why it's important for us to talk about  inclusion  how we as leaders can foster inclusive  working environments for our teams but  what exactly do I mean by that well an  inclusive working environment is one in  which everyone feels respected and  connected to each other simple as that  now there's an ever growing body of  evidence which shows that LGBTQ people  who do not feel included in the  workplace are less productive and are at  greater risk of leaving furthermore  companies that do not appear to be  inclusive from the outside struggle more  to recruit LGBTQ talent and to give you  a sense of the numbers here by some  estimates there are as many as 16  million LGBTQ people in this country's  workforce alone 16 million now I'm a  Kiwi and granted New Zealand's a small  country but that's like four times our  population like in 2016 after I'd come  out at work I was building up an LGBTQ  employee network there and I approached  our security team to ask them whether  they were factoring in LGBTQ  considerations into their risk  assessments of new country entries you  see I worked in the oil and gas industry  and many of the countries that my  company was looking to move into how  particularly hostile views toward LGBTQ  people well that question led our  security team to realize that they were  sending or we're at least trying to send  LGBTQ people to countries that  criminalize homosexuality with  punishments of jail time and even death  now as a leader can you imagine coming  to that realization the realization that  your actions or the decisions that you  have made could unwittingly have put one  of your team members at risk that's a  scary thought now in front of me today I  see a bunch of young aspiring leaders I  see people who are going to have to deal  with challenges like this in the few  and who will be leading some of those 16  million LGBTQ Americans and countless  others from around the world and while  it's easy for us as leaders to focus on  the hard numbers in business if we truly  want to develop healthy high-functioning  cohesive teams then it's our  responsibility to focus on inclusion so  I'd like to leave you with three  challenges today my first challenge to  you is be mindful of the signals that  you are sending let's rewind a bit to  the story I told you about Chris and I  when Chris spoke to me about my partner  he used the word girlfriend it was a  phrasing he probably didn't think twice  about but it left me feeling as though I  couldn't be opened with him as though he  had some kind of like predetermined  ideal for what a team member and his  team would look like and that that was a  mold that I just didn't fit into had  Chris simply used the word partner or  something of that nature it would have  made me feel much more comfortable in  that moment my second challenge to you  is have the courage to make mistakes  when I came out to a colleague in at  work a couple of years ago his first  words to me were yeah I thought so he  then went to Google and typed in what to  do when someone at work comes out to me  and yeah I thought so was one of the top  things that Google said not to say  but hey I brushed it off and he didn't  allow it to discourage him himself  instead he used it as an opportunity to  learn and now when I asked how he would  respond to a similar situation in the  future his response is a simple  I appreciate you opening up to me and  that's a great start  my third challenge to you today is lead  with empathy now just for a second I  want everyone in this room to think  about your deepest darkest secret  something that no one else in the world  knows I want you now to think about what  it would feel like to tell someone that  secret for the very first time  your heart is absolutely racing and your  palms are sweating  it's literally a fight-or-flight  response this is how it felt for me to  come out to my manager back in 2014 so I  want you to put yourself in the shoes of  those who follow you and to think about  how they perceive you as a leader do you  lean into emotional situations like this  and look for an opportunity to connect  or do you shy away are you open and  approachable  someone to whom your team members can  really talk about anything or are you  closed off now I'll admit it's easy for  me to stand up here and offer you these  challenges in the liberal Enclave that  is the Bay Area and that there are some  parts of the world where I could be  prosecuted for just giving this talk  while I think it's important for us all  to exercise sound judgment I want to  remind you of one thing being inclusive  is simply about making people feel  respected and connected and how you  achieve that is essentially the same  regardless of whether you've got someone  in your team who is LGBTQ identifying or  whether there's something else that  makes them unique when I step back  and I put my myself in Chris's shoes  back in 2012 I can see that he was just  doing what felt natural to him at the  time but as leaders we need to  prioritize inclusion and put the needs  of our team members first so I challenge  you to be mindful of the signals that  you are sending to have the courage to  make mistakes and to lead with empathy  all in an effort to create healthy high  functioning teams and organizations so  my question to all of you now is are you  all up for the challenge thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

